WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.499
Self-consistency: ask several times, trust agreement.

2
00:00:03.779 --> 00:00:08.941
A signal that holds up better than self-reported confidence is agreement across samples.

3
00:00:09.221 --> 00:00:20.571
Wang and colleagues (2022) introduced self-consistency for reasoning: sample several independent chains of thought instead of one, then take the answer most of them reach.

4
00:00:20.851 --> 00:00:27.997
On math word problems it raised accuracy substantially (the paper reports gains as large as 17.

5
00:00:28.277 --> 00:00:31.093
9 points on the GSM8K benchmark).

6
00:00:31.373 --> 00:00:36.792
The intuition: a correct answer can usually be reached in several ways, while errors scatter.

7
00:00:37.072 --> 00:00:42.341
For extraction, the analog is to run the same extraction several times and compare.

8
00:00:42.621 --> 00:00:49.597
An action item that appears in five of five runs, with the same owner, is more trustworthy than one that appears once.

9
00:00:49.877 --> 00:00:58.197
Disagreement is the useful part: if three runs say the owner is Sam and two say nobody, that item deserves a person’s attention.

10
00:00:58.477 --> 00:01:09.016
Anthropic’s hallucination guidance suggests the same idea, calling it best-of-N verification: inconsistencies across outputs can indicate hallucination.

11
00:01:09.296 --> 00:01:10.363
Two caveats.

12
00:01:10.643 --> 00:01:18.536
Agreement isn’t truth: a model can be consistently wrong, especially when the transcript is ambiguous in the same way every time.

13
00:01:18.816 --> 00:01:27.328
And it multiplies cost by the number of samples, so use it selectively (for high-stakes fields, or for items that already failed a cheap check).

14
00:01:27.608 --> 00:01:39.939
Matching items across runs is also real work: two runs may phrase the same task differently, so match on evidence quotes or on owner plus a normalized task, not on exact task text.
